    description: "Follow-up to WI-ronot (closed 2026-04-17). On ripgrep, enabling procMacro.enable + procMacro.attributes.enable + cargo.buildScripts.enable produced a BYTE-IDENTICAL SCIP symbol set — proc-macros are loaded but not surfaced as SCIP symbols. Caveat: ripgrep has modest proc-macro usage.\n\nGate question (needs human review before we spend time): is it worth re-testing this on proc-macro-heavy crates (a clap-Parser-based CLI, a wasm_bindgen-heavy app, a tauri app) before committing to keep per-macro pattern code in wasm_bindgen.py / napi.py / tauri_ipc.py linkers? If rust-analyzer's behavior turns out to depend on proc-macro density, the linker story simplifies materially on some corpora. If not, we've confirmed the ripgrep result generalises and can stop checking.\n\nCost of investigation: ~1-2 hours. Install rust-analyzer on 2-3 proc-macro-heavy crates, run with/without procMacro.enable, diff the symbol sets. Same methodology as WI-ronot.\n\nWhy low-priority-needs-review rather than todo_soft: the ripgrep result is already informative enough to proceed with the current linker design; the open question is whether we should additionally verify the generalisation before closing this line of inquiry."  # 81ab
